# FFX.exe symbol map: the source of truth for names we have recovered.
# Target: FFX.exe sha256 0537b2a1047f3266e73495cd4e35f63f0777f4231d417699f979954686da686d
# Format: <address> <function|label> <name> [# comment]
# Keep sorted by address. Names: Module_Verb or Class_Method; globals g_Name.
#
# Lines whose comment starts with "fh " are the Fahrenheit team's names, merged
# by tools/fh-import.py (used with written permission from peppy, 2026-10-16).
# The comment gives the origin: v4 = named in their database of this build,
# v3 = carried over from their older Steam build (exact or BSim match, with the
# v3 address), switch = Switch build symbol (accepted or BSim match, with the
# Switch address), call.cs = their hand-checked FhCall table (with the calling
# convention). The original C++ name follows when the token differs. BSim
# matches are the least certain: confirm before relying on one. The script
# rewrites these lines on each run; to keep one as ours, drop the "fh " prefix
# from its comment. Where both projects had named a function we took their
# name; "was <name>" gives our old one. "fh: <name>" after our own comment is
# their (different) name for it.
